  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Nor'easter brings below normal temperatures,\nbreezy conditions, and some rain at times through this weekend.\n\nA Nor'easter is positioned east of New Jersey early this morning\nbeneath a closed upper low. These features will drift west or\nsouthwest today as an upper level ridge passes across eastern\nCanada. Showers along the Chesapeake Bay will continue to expand\nwestward through the day. There remains some uncertainty with\njust how far south and west the rain will reach due to\nrelatively drier air. Winds are also a bit more northwesterly\ntoday, so some downsloping could help erode the western edge as\nwell. Rain amounts will be light, generally less than a quarter\ninch for most, although locations in northeastern Maryland could\nsee up to an additional inch of rain. Expect wind gusts to\nincrease again after sunrise as the gradient wind field rotates\nover the area. Peak gusts will again be in the 25-35 mph range\nfor much of the area. The advancing rain and lower clouds will\nlimit temperatures for the northern two thirds of the area in\nthe 60s. Locations in central Virginia will likely rise into the\nlower to mid 70s. Tidal flooding will continue to be an issue\nthrough tonight...see separate section below.\n\nThe Nor'easter will start weakening tonight as it pivots near\nthe Delaware Bay. This will lead to decreasing rain chances\ntonight, although some patches of light rain or drizzle may\npersist. Lows will be in the 50s.\n\nAs the blocking ridge departs, the low will be able to start\nprogressing northeastward Monday while continuing to weaken.\nWinds gusts will be much lower than the weekend, and most\nlocations should remain dry. However, cloud cover will likely \nremain stubbornly in place in a broad moisture shield left\nbehind by the upper low. The current forecast calls for highs in\nthe upper 60s and lower 70s, but would not be surprised if they\nare a bit lower if clouds are thick. As winds shift more \nnorthwesterly, some upslope showers or drizzle will be possible \nin the Alleghenies.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and tranquil weather is expected for the\nmiddle of the week before rain chances return by late in the \nwork week.\n\nAs the Nor'easter and accompanying longwave trough continue to \npull away from the Atlantic coast Tuesday and Wednesday, \nmid/upper heights begin to build in earnest. These above average\n500-mb heights are forecast to run around 585-588 dm as the \ntransient upper ridge swings eastward toward the Atlantic \nSeaboard. Subsidence accompanying this regime favors dry \nconditions. However, the moisture left behind by the low may\nbecome trapped beneath the subsidence inversion, so Tuesday will\nlikely feature more clouds than sun. More sun is likely\nWednesday as surface winds turn southerly. Some fair weather \ncumulus likely develop Wednesday and Thursday as instability \nincreases a bit through the day. However, the forecast is dry \nthrough at least Thursday.\n\nThe peak in the warming trend is likely to occur in the mid/late \nweek period. Global ensemble box-and-whisker plots show fairly low \nspread on Wednesday and Thursday and high temperatures return to the \nlow to perhaps mid 80s. Much of the late week uncertainty revolves \naround the increase in shower and cloud coverage. There is some\nuncertainty whether the front pushes southward rather quickly or\nstalls out nearby. Once this system does push southward, \ntemperatures are expected to return to more seasonable numbers \nfor next weekend.",
